## 🔥 The most important production concept

**Do NOT load the model inside the route.**

Don't do this:

```python
@app.route("/")
def home():

    model = LinerRegModel11()

    model.load_state_dict(...)

    ...
```

That would potentially reload the model every time someone requests the page.

Instead:

```python
model = LinerRegModel11()
model.load_state_dict(...)
model.eval()
```

Load it **once when the Flask application starts**.

Then every request uses the already-loaded model:

```text
Flask starts
    ↓
Load model ONCE
    ↓
Request 1 → prediction
Request 2 → prediction
Request 3 → prediction
Request 4 → prediction
```

That's the basic pattern you'll eventually use for your **Student Risk Predictor, AgriLink ML models, and other ML-powered Flask applications**.

And one more thing: if your training data was **scaled/normalized**, Flask must apply the **exact same preprocessing** before sending the input to PyTorch. That's one of the biggest things to get right when moving an ML model into a web app.
